# Files in this repo that are NOT part of the published site.
#
# The assets directory is the repository root (the same directory Netlify
# published), so this file is the whole answer to "what is downloadable" — it
# replaces netlify.toml's nine 404 rules. On Cloudflare nothing here is uploaded
# at all, which is a stronger statement than refusing it at the CDN: a path that
# never reached the edge cannot be served by a rule that was later edited away.
#
# It is also, unlike the old redirect rules, one engine with one glob syntax
# (gitignore's), so check-freshness.mjs reads it directly and fails the suite
# when a published file is neither an app asset nor listed here — and when a
# rule here would exclude something the app itself loads (sw.js's SHELL list).
# The rules that used to be written one-per-path because Netlify's redirect
# globbing could not express them — the docs, the README imagery, the two
# harnesses, the spikes — are now single lines with the same effect.

# Repo plumbing and local tooling.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.freebuff/
.wrangler/
.netlify/
node_modules/

# The function sources, the config, and the Pages Function that reaches them.
# Pages compiles functions/ from the repository at deploy time, so the sources
# are not needed in the upload — and publishing them would hand out the sync
# proxy's validation logic and every env var name (what netlify.toml's rules
# refused).
netlify/
functions/
wrangler.toml
_headers
.assetsignore

# Local secrets for `wrangler dev` — the same file .gitignore refuses.
.dev.vars

# The guard suite: laptop-run, and its scripts read the deploy's own config.
scripts/
testing/

# README imagery, and the docs themselves: they carry the install steps, the
# secret names and the hosting model. A bare name matches at any depth, so
# README.md covers vendor/README.md too — which had its own Netlify rule for
# exactly this reason.
screenshots/
README.md
CLAUDE.md
package.json
package-lock.json

# The local dev server: both run on a laptop and
# neither has any business being downloadable.
serve.sh
# The accessibility harness: it drives the real app in an iframe, so it is a
# laptop tool like the roster harness above and never part of the deploy.
a11y-harness.html

# Data-shaped artefacts must never be served, even committed by accident: an
# exported CSV or a pulled cloud copy is somebody's real spending, and the
# assets directory would otherwise publish it at the same URL as the app.
*.csv
*.xlsx
data*.json

# The new Cents app: the cutover target. Its source, typed logic core and
# tsconfig are development files while the old root app serves, so none of it
# may be downloadable. The cutover commit removes this line together with the
# flip to app/ as the assets directory — the two must move as one.
app/

# Standard noise
.DS_Store
*.log
*.tlog
*.swp
*.swo
.idea/
.vscode/
.env
.env.*
# Deploy files: the script that stages the upload and the staging output it
# writes (`.pages-dist/`) are development files. The assets directory is the
# repository root, so anything not listed here would be uploaded and served.
.pages-dist/
tools/
